// Regular expressions as JavaScript writes them, without its slashes:
// `regex_matches(t, "^\\d{4}-\\d{2}", "")`. A pattern is followed every
// way at once (Pike's machine, in devices/regex.c), so it takes time in
// proportion to the text times the pattern, whatever the pattern: one
// from a file cannot make a program hang. Where it may match in more
// ways, the match is the one JavaScript finds: the leftmost, then the
// alternatives and greedy or lazy repeats in their order.
//
// Read: characters (UTF-8, one code point each), `.`, classes `[a-z]`
// `[^...]`, `\d \w \s` and `\D \W \S`, `^ $`, `\b \B`, groups `( )`
// `(?: )` `(?<name> )`, `|`, repeats `* + ? {n} {n,} {n,m}` (lazy with a
// `?` after), the escapes `\n \r \t \f \v \0 \xHH \uHHHH`, and `\` before
// a sign. Flags: `i` (A to Z as a to z), `m` (`^ $` of each line), `s`
// (`.` takes a line's end too); `g u y d` are taken and change nothing.
// Not read: back references (`\1`) and looking ahead or behind (`(?=`
// `(?!` `(?<=` `(?<!`), which need going back; such a pattern is wrong
// (`regex_error` says why) and matches nothing.
//
// One way it differs from JavaScript: where a repeated group can match
// nothing and is lazy inside (`(?:a*?)+`), JavaScript leaves out a round
// that matched nothing and goes on, and the match it gives may end
// later; whether there is a match is the same. (20,000 random patterns
// compared with node's: no other difference.)
//
// A repeat counts to 1000 at most; a pattern makes a program of 50000
// steps at most (repeats of repeats multiply).
import text

// Why `pattern` (with `flags`) is no pattern: "" where it is one.
extern fn regex_error(pattern: text, flags: text) -> text
// Whether `pattern` matches somewhere in `t` (as JavaScript's `test`).
extern fn regex_matches(t: text, pattern: text, flags: text) -> bool
// Where the first match from byte `from` on starts, and where it ends
// (the byte after it); -1 where there is none.
extern fn regex_find(t: text, pattern: text, flags: text, from: i32) -> i32
extern fn regex_end(t: text, pattern: text, flags: text, from: i32) -> i32
// What group `group` of that match matched (the groups are the `( )`
// and `(?<name> )`, numbered from 1 as their "(" come; 0 is the whole
// match): "" where there is no match or the group matched nothing.
extern fn regex_group(t: text, pattern: text, flags: text, from: i32, group: i32) -> text
